From ba6f302193d48c998af744b25d5f4e93c9ad1deb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yankneck696 <yankneck696@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 06:43:20 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 03/14] PureSignal: keep the feedback alignment cheap over a
 networked radio's long history (#525)

The feedback is processed on the engine thread that feeds the
transmitter, and over the second of history a networked Pluto needs, two
parts of it had grown expensive enough to hold that thread up (full
searches of 27 ms, measured on a PlutoSky R2 at 2.5 Msps):

- The periodic alignment check searched the whole history even while
  locked. Harmless over the tenth of a second a LimeSDR keeps; over the
  second a networked Pluto needs, a burst long enough to hold up the
  transmit feed every half second. Locked, it now searches 128 samples either
  side of the held alignment and keeps it if it still scores; a delay that
  has jumped further fails that, and the full search runs on the next block.
- The LO difference was spun out with a sine and cosine per sample. A
  running double-precision rotation gives the same result (closer, in fact:
  the old code rounded the phase itself to single precision) for a fraction
  of the cost.

After a loss of lock, the next search looks near the alignment last held
(about 50 ms either side at 2.5 Msps) before searching the whole history,
since a gap in either stream moves the delay a little, not anywhere. The
full search takes its cross terms from an FFT, and the full-rate refinement
works out each envelope once instead of at every position. Measured on the
reference history of a 2.5 Msps Pluto: the full search went from 39 ms to
2.8 ms, and the search near a held alignment takes 0.07 ms.

This is the shared predistorter, so the LimeSDR-USB's PureSignal takes the
same path. Tests check the rotation against the exact phase, the sliding
and FFT searches against correlating at every lag, that a held alignment survives
the narrow re-check while a jumped delay is found again, and that a small
jump is found near the last alignment.
