From 9ae6aed37023500fe455d0be71c239b8ad2aa19f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yankneck696 <yankneck696@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 14:07:43 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 08/14] PlutoSDR: send transmit buffers of at most 32768
 samples, so a board streaming receive too keeps up (#525)

On a PlutoSky R2 in 2R2T over Gigabit Ethernet at 2.5 Msps, with both
receive chains and transmit streaming, the transmit stream stopped for
about 300 ms every few seconds: the board's eth0 dropped incoming packets
(its receive FIFO error count rose by about 1024 an over) and each lost one
waited out the PC's minimum retransmission timeout, 300 ms on Windows.

The same happens without sdroxide. libiio's own iio_readdev (both chains)
and iio_writedev, at 61440 samples per buffer, about what the 25 ms target
gives at that rate: 1023 packets dropped in a minute, and 60 s of transmit
took 61.06 s. At 32768 samples: 135 dropped and 60.35 s. Either alone
drops none. Pacing sdroxide's writes in 16 KiB pieces did not help, so it
is how much the board takes in at once that matters.

The transmit buffer is now capped at 32768 samples (13 ms at 2.5 Msps).
On the R2 with this, a minute's over with PureSignal on ran without a
stall once under way (the longest transmit gap 38 ms, each buffer sent in
under 19 ms, eth0 dropping 137), with the table steady at -1.7 to -1.8 dB.
A few stalls remain in the first seconds after key-up.

13 ms still outlasts the 2.2 ms round trip measured over a Pluto's USB
gadget by six times. The buffer-length test now checks three times that
measured round trip, up to 4 Msps, instead of three times a 5 ms allowance
up to 5 Msps, which a 32768 cap cannot meet even at 2.5 Msps; it also checks
the cap at the default rate.
