From 3ae54ed651d90857d365f661699749e316abb137 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yankneck696 <yankneck696@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 14:58:19 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 09/14] PlutoSDR PureSignal: drop transmit blocks rather than
 let the queue grow past 200 ms (#525)

When the link stalls, the engine keeps producing and the host's transmit
ring fills. The transmit thread then drains it only at the sample rate, so
the backlog never shrinks again: each stall lengthens the loop for the rest
of the over. The ring holds up to 0.8 s at 2.5 Msps, and with the board's
buffers and the network on top that passes the second of history
PureSignal looks back over.

The test builds the PureSignal hardware results came from (a steady table
through stalls, overs of a minute and more) dropped blocks past 200 ms of
queue, but the bound was never carried into these commits. On a PlutoSky R2
the commits without it, in one over with no other change, went from
1.8 dB of correction at a 0.97 match to 4.1 dB and then 15.3 dB at 0.72
within 40 s. This adds it: while PureSignal is on, a block that would queue
past 200 ms is dropped before predistortion, so nothing dropped enters the
reference. A steady over queues 130-160 ms and drops nothing.
---
