From 4e55f95b3ab59f4e656d6bc0075be77442f85792 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yankneck696 <yankneck696@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 06:38:03 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 10/14] PlutoSDR RX2: never take a pair's first half as
 unpaired, and switch lockstep on only with the chain attached (#525)

From the review of #743. rx_read_paired read the pairing origin, found it
unset, and then drained chain 0's ring without a limit. If the receive
thread recorded the origin and committed the first pair in between, that
pair's chain-0 half went out as unpaired history, and every paired read
after it handed RX2 up to a buffer behind RX1, for good: the diversity null
never forms, and nothing reports it.

The ring is now measured before the origin is read, and an unpaired read
takes no more than that. The receive thread records the origin before it
commits the first pair, so whatever was waiting when the origin still read
as unset is all from before it. The limit is worked out in unpaired_room(),
with a unit test; the old code's limit was the whole buffer.

aux_rx switched lockstep on before rx(1) and off again if that failed. When
chain 1 was already running as another radio, the receive thread could run
that radio's ring in lockstep for the moment in between. Lockstep is now
switched on inside the attach, under the lock that hands the ring to the
receive thread and only once the chain is ours, so the thread never sees the
new ring without it, nor lockstep without the new ring.

The loopback fake can now count in both chains' Q, the same count for the
two halves of one instant, so a pair read out of step shows as differing
counts; the constant patterns could not show that. A new test borrows and
returns RX2 twelve times, reading pairs from the moment of each borrow, and
checks every pair's counts match. It passes on the old code too: the window
is too narrow to hit reliably from a test, which is why the limit itself has
the unit test.
